{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-102304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $4y+12{y}^{′}=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2,…?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+12{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+12{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the second summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+12{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[4{a}_{n}+12(n+1){a}_{n+1}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n4{a}_{n}+12(n+1){a}_{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)},\\,n=0,1,2,….\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-102304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{(4n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{12(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=12{a}_{n}(n+1)"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3{a}_{n}}{(n+1)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{′}-4y=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2,…?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[3(n+1){a}_{n+1}-4{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n3(n+1){a}_{n+1}-4{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{4{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)},\\,n=0,1,2,….\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-101381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-12{a}_{n}(n+1)"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{4{a}_{n}}{3}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=4{a}_{n}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{12{a}_{n}}{(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{4{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101381" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{4{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{′}-y=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2,…?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[3(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n3(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)},\\,n=0,1,2,….\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-64512" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64512" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-3(n+1){a}_{n}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{3{a}_{n}}{n}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}+3}{n}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3n}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64512" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-365775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{′}+5y=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2…?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-365775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+5{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+5{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+5{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}+5{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n(n+1){a}_{n+1}+5{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{5{a}_{n}}{n+1},\\,n=0,1,2….\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-365775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/1