) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[4(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4(n+1)},\\,n=0,1,2….\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-365781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-365781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-365781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=4{a}_{n}(n+1)"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{4{a}_{n}}{n+1}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4n}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-365781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4(n+1)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3xy-2{y}^{′}=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}-{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }2(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }2(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}-2{a}_{1}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3{a}_{n-1}-2(n+1){a}_{n+1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,2(n+1){a}_{n+1}=3{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-102312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-5{a}_{n-1}(n+1)"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-6{a}_{n-1}(n+1)"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{2{a}_{n-1}}{3n+1}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2n+1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102312" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{′}+7xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+7x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+7{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+7{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+7{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+7{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+7{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}+7{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-7{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{7{a}_{n-1}}{n+1},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-64536" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64536" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-7(n+1){a}_{n-1}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-7n{a}_{n-1}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{7{a}_{n-1}}{n+1}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=7n{a}_{n}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{7(n+1)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64536" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{7{a}_{n-1}}{n+1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $6xy+2{y}^{′}=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}+{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }2(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }2(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}+2{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n2{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6{a}_{n-1}+2(n+1){a}_{n+1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,2(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-6{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-102317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{(3n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-{a}_{n-1}(3n+1)"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=3{a}_{n-1}(n+1)"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{3(n+1)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{3{a}_{n-1}}{(n+1)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $6{y}^{′}+3xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+3x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }6(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }6(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n6{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n6{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(n+1){a}_{n+1}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,6(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-3{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-101402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-2{a}_{n}(n+1)"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{2n+1}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-{a}_{n-1}(n+1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $4{y}^{′}+xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }4(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }4(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n4{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n4{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[4(n+1){a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,4(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{4(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-64541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-4n{a}_{n-1}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{4(n+1)}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{4n+1}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=4(n+1){a}_{n-1}"] ["math" "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{4{a}_{n-1}}{n+1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{4(n+1)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{″}-2y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}-2{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}-2{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}-2{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-2{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-2{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{2{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-101385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(n)=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}"] ["math" "f(n)=\\frac{2}{3(n+1)(n+2)}"] ["math" "f(n)=\\frac{2}{3n(n+2)}"] ["math" "f(n)=\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}"] ["math" "f(n)=\\frac{3n(n+1)}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-101385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(n)=\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $6y+9{y}^{″}=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+9{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+9{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the second summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+9{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[6{a}_{n}+9(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n6{a}_{n}+9(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=-\\frac{2{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=-\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-102309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(n)=-(2n+1)(3n+2)"] ["math" "f(n)=-6(n+1)(n+2)"] ["math" "f(n)=-\\frac{2(n+1)(n+2)}{3}"] ["math" "f(n)=-\\frac{2}{3(n+1)(n+2)}"] ["math" "f(n)=-\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(n)=-\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{″}+y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}+{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}+{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=-3(n+1)(n+2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-64531" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64531" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(n)=-3(n+1)(n+2)"] ["math" "f(n)=-3n(3n+1)"] ["math" "f(n)=-n(n+3)"] ["math" "f(n)=3(n+2)(n+3)"] ["math" "f(n)=n(2n+3)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-64531" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(n)=-3(n+1)(n+2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-365780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{″}-y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-365780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=3(n+1)(n+2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-365780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-365780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-365780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(n)=(n+1)(n+2)"] ["math" "f(n)=3(n+1)(2n+1)"] ["math" "f(n)=3(n+1)(n+2)"] ["math" "f(n)=3(n+1)(n+3)"] ["math" "f(n)=3n(n+2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-365780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(n)=3(n+1)(n+2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $y+{y}^{″}=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the second summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[{a}_{n}+(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}+(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=-(n+1)(n+2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc220446208fa6f8f92146eb0dc398c1977eaf106cd9f2aa67df1d40e8ec59c2"} {"question" "q-102307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(n)=(n+1)(n+2)"] ["math" "f(n)=-(2n+1)(n+2)"] ["math" "f(n)=-(n+1)(n+2)"] ["math" "f(n)=-\\frac{1}{(n+1)(n+2)}"] ["math" "f(n)=\\frac{1}{(n+1)(n+2)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "q-102307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(n)=-(n+1)(n+2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/state.json" "file_sha256" "ead605bdde65c7d6ec6fd4a8a04f47fd5fcd4a280954709d80e3ed288277fde8"} {"question" "e-6671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y = \\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $y'-3y=0$.\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_n$, where $n=0,1,2, \\ldots$?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/example-6671.json" "file_sha256" "29edfef35e899a667a18ade26fa0db095130aef5077aa903f26c8cfd2f20dc12"} {"question" "e-6671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $y'(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y'(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty n a_n x^{n-1}-3\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n = 0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+1) a_{m+1} x^{m} -3 \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n =0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+1) a_{n+1} x^{n} -3 \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n =0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0$, we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-3{a}_{n}{x}^{n}] & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}-3{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n(n+1) a_{n+1}  -3 a_n = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} =\\dfrac{3 a_n }{n+1}, \\qquad n= 0,1,2, \\ldots\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/example-6671.json" "file_sha256" "29edfef35e899a667a18ade26fa0db095130aef5077aa903f26c8cfd2f20dc12"} {"question" "e-6673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/2ad87a5ee9c27f6d3f03b098b804613d81bc91bb5a811b7655e12efcb052f1e8.png) be a series solution to the equation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/5379e686b1ac80c3cb5fec6c41ae2e281b7db590044ff70ff9c3068ac1332f98.png)\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coeffi