ange the index of the summation by writing ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/3ec632b09d057774b1bde29312445fcd718265d1e3a7cc9e0bb6454b1278ddba.png) This gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/14db9e7def07eb3ddc1cc681bd205c8f218bcb30607c2ee331c13cef44cc474c.png)\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/b923127d389ef7844a2cad7324f58bbb44721e77945a168273e7d58fb06cfa46.png) This gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/cd14f7084c35b6f6e98a194cefc6e80eecd5b8d4ba975851fc1bab5861f2bb13.png)\n\nReplacing ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/c07274e7bcff0fa7fb2912ede95bbc9ba7c31af65b3e4fc0613174ca843bccc4.png) with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/56de185c0e7e838b89aec2ec1cee1ebc90029112047f9cebc42de13a1bcf3f95.png) in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/fe5b5c122194b65330b9c7bbd1530ded53b90f4bdd98d79dd5794942e08599a5.png)\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/412e3e48aabdabf0dbdfce8e5b8e6e24c7558d50640bfb31ad4b642d9de39ff3.png) So, we take out the term ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/63890006ecc80706f3bce27717772762e7fb252410047a230519f4d926b5b988.png) from the left-hand side:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/9111081928de5b15aeca089185c43c60a70d9ca2f0eb96c9872ab04204b16297.png)\n\nWe now combine the series as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/bc7ab05dbf97481578fcc8352d76608b3cfe2b7b4301af51eadd810c160a247b.png)\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/977bbc80efee8cdaf6667c4ced8e836d3fb77c53e232065541b1ed72594cada3.png) to zero, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/04082d8431a1c86db2282e85065a089bd3cb18082ae278d03831bcde599e56f0.png)\n\nSolving for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/fc1f5e662d05224ca25f4893c62389cc5d8b11acbae73f269a7921e097374b85.png) gives\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/683610152cf39c63fcf62ccdeadf1beed4ff8ef1cfb6a28452420974901cdd0c.png)\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/7ce940ebe652683318187b453d18127a4334b5c0dfef68da5e9a74e937f2d734.png)\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index equals ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/0021ff77f0c4565f235da704e0b4d45d1145486e46d721ea834cba941061b020.png) since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/assets/ea7eeb2aa3909170b1ed83f408f8b17ec17d5012db54fd49910e85034899dbb8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/example-6673.json" "file_sha256" "49b05c7d8b319973886bcd31dfed3c5c45ae5be67d895fde6577a1ce618c2f2d"} {"question" "e-6672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $2y''+y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$$\na_{n+2} = \\dfrac{ a_n }{ f(n) }, \\qquad n= 0,1,2\\ldots\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/example-6672.json" "file_sha256" "43bc1c1fc41c83abdcf136f5605f3556a0752d012a61bc6b7e0d059001b6da4d"} {"question" "e-6672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $y'(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find $y''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y''(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty n (n-1) a_{n} x^{n-2} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+2) (m+1) a_{m+2} x^{m} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+2) (n+1) a_{n+2} x^{n} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[2(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}+{a}_{n}{x}^{n}] & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[2(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}+{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n2(n+2)(n+1) a_{n+2}  +a_n = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+2} = -\\dfrac{ a_n }{ 2(n+1)(n+2)  }, \\qquad n= 0,1,2 \\ldots\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(n)=-2(n+1)(n+2).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042594/example-6672.json" "file_sha256" "43bc1c1fc41c83abdcf136f5605f3556a0752d012a61bc6b7e0d059001b6da4d"}] "usage" {"e-6671" [] "e-6672" [] "q-64541" [] "e-6673" []} "basis" 1562 "retractions" [[17592186148115 :question/problem "Let $y = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $4y^{′} + xy = 0$.\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_{n}$, where $n = 1, 2, 3, …$"] [17592186147310 :question/problem "Let $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $y'-3y=0$.\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_n$, where $n=0,1,2 \\ldots$?"] [17592186147310 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $y'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(x) &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= (a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= 0 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y'(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} - 3∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(m = 0)^(∞)(m + 1)a_{m + 1}x^{m} - 3∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(n + 1)a_{n + 1}x^{n} - 3∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0$, we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(n + 1)a_{n + 1}x^{n} - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)3a_{n}x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)[(n + 1)a_{n + 1}x^{n} - 3a_{n}x^{n}] &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)[(n + 1)a_{n + 1} - 3a_{n}]x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n(n+1) a_{n+1} -3 a_n = 0\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} =\\dfrac{3 a_n}{n+1}, \\qquad n= 0,1,2 \\ldots\n$$"] [17592186147312 :question/problem "Let $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $y' +4xy = 0$.\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_n$, where $n=1,2,3 \\ldots$"] [17592186147312 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $y'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(x) &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= (a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y'(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} + 4x∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} + ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)4a_{n}x^{n + 1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+1) a_{m+1} x^{m} + \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty 4a_n x^{n+1} =0\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing\n\n$$\nm = n+1\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+1) a_{m+1} x^{m} + \\sum_{m=1}^\\infty 4a_{m-1} x^{m} =0\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+1) a_{n+1} x^{n} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty 4 a_{n-1} x^{n} =0\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n$. So, we take out the term $n = 0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\na_1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (n+1) a_{n+1} x^{n} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty 4a_{n-1} x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(n + 1)a_{n + 1}x^{n} + 4a_{n - 1}x^{n}] &= 0 \\\\\na_{1} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(n + 1)a_{n + 1} + 4a_{n - 1}]x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of $x^n$, we obtain\n\n$$\na_1 = 0, \\qquad (n+1) a_{n+1} = -4a_{n-1}\n$$\n\nSolving for $a_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = -\\dfrac{4a_{n-1}}{n+1}, \\quad n=1,2,3 \\ldots\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = -\\dfrac{4a_{n-1}}{n+1}, \\quad a_1=0, \\quad n=1,2,3 \\ldots\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index equals to $0$, since $a_1=0$."] [17592186147311 :question/problem "Let $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $2y''+y=0$. Find $f(n)$, given that the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$a_{n+2} = \\dfrac{a_n}{f(n)}, \\qquad n= 0,1,2\\ldots$"] [17592186147311 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $y'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(x) &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= (a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= 0 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find $y''(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″}(x) &= (∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1})^{′} \\\\\n&= (a_{1} + ∑_(n = 2)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1})^{′} \\\\\n&= 0 + ∑_(n = 2)^(∞)n(n - 1)a_{n}x^{n - 2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 2)^(∞)n(n - 1)a_{n}x^{n - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y''(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty n (n-1) a_{n} x^{n-2} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-2$. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+2) (m+1) a_{m+2} x^{m} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+2) (n+1) a_{n+2} x^{n} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0$, we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)2(n + 2)(n + 1)a_{n + 2}x^{n} + ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)[2(n + 2)(n + 1)a_{n + 2}x^{n} + a_{n}x^{n}] &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)[2(n + 2)(n + 1)a_{n + 2} + a_{n}]x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n2(n+2)(n+1) a_{n+2} +a_n = 0\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+2} = -\\dfrac{a_n}{2(n+1)(n+2)}, \\qquad n= 0,1,2 \\ldots\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(n)=-2(n+1)(n+2)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186146702 17592186146703 17592186146704 17592186146705 17592186146706] "immutable_fields" [[17592186146701 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146701 :answer-field/id] [17592186146701 :answer-field/key] [17592186146701 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
